%description:

Tcp.minshallNagle:on -- full-sized segments in flight do not hold the short end of a write.

The claim (Tcp.ned): "with nagleEnabled, the variant of Minshall, as Linux
(tcp_minshall_check()): a small segment waits only while an earlier small segment is not
acknowledged, so a flight of full-sized segments does not hold the end of a write".

The classes: off (false, the default), on (true; LinuxTcp). The code: with Minshall, only an
earlier small segment that is not acknowledged holds a small segment
(TcpConnection::sendData()). This test separates "on" from "off".

Host under test: host1, with Nagle, which writes 3000 bytes at once; the round trip is 40 ms.
Observation: level 1, the end of 104 bytes goes at once with the full-sized segments.

%# parameter: Tcp.minshallNagle
%# class: on
%# value: true
%# key: *.host1.tcp.minshallNagle
%# expected-result: PASS

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("TcpMinshallNagleOn")
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
             .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == false && pk.dataLength > tcp.headerLength")
             .describe("the first data segment of host1")
             .within(0.5))
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
             .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == false && pk.dataLength > tcp.headerLength && pk.dataLength < tcp.headerLength + 1448B")
             .describe("the end of the write, at once")
             .within(0.005));
}

%activity:
EV << "TcpMinshallNagleOn compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host1.tcp.minshallNagle = true    # the parameter under test
*.linkDelay = 10ms
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 3000B
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST TcpMinshallNagleOn: PASS
